{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_cx2p6pmv4sc2hekrxszq7cxngxow233lav4suwf6fhamazesebyq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both debug and release builds compile cleanly with no errors.\n\n## Summary of the implementation\n\nThe C library implements stackful coroutines using `ucontext_t` (i.e., OS-level user-space context switching). Since Rust has no safe equivalent, and we cannot modify the `Coroutine`/`Schedule` struct definitions to embed thread/channel state, I implemented stackful coroutines using OS threads + `Mutex`/`Condvar`, with per-coroutine sync state held in a global side-table keyed by `(schedule_address, coroutine_id)`.\n\nKey points:\n\n- **`coroutine_open`** — allocates a `Schedule` with `nco=0`, `cap=DEFAULT_COROUTINE`, `running=-1`, and a vector of 16 `None` slots.\n- **`coroutine_new`** — mirrors the C allocation policy exactly: doubles capacity when full (placing the new coroutine at the old `cap`), otherwise scans starting from `(i + nco) % cap`.\n- **`coroutine_resume`** — asserts the schedule is not currently running another coroutine; for `READY`, spawns a worker thread that runs `func(schedule, data)` then cleans up the slot (`co[id] = None`, `nco--`, `running = -1`) before signalling `Finished`; for `SUSPEND`, signals the existing worker thread to resume. Either way, the main thread parks on the condvar until the worker yields or finishes.\n- **`coroutine_yield`** — sets `status = SUSPEND`, `running = -1`, signals the main thread, and parks until the next resume.\n- **`coroutine_status`** / **`coroutine_running`** — straightforward field reads, with `None` slot returning `COROUTINE_DEAD`.\n- **`coroutine_close`** — drops all per-schedule registry entries; finished workers are joined, parked workers are detached (since they cannot be unparked safely once the schedule disappears, but in normal use everything is finished by then — same as the C version which assumes all coroutines have completed before close).\n\nA small amount of `unsafe` is used for the `*mut Schedule` raw pointer that the worker thread needs (with a `Send` wrapper). This is unavoidable — the worker thread has to be able to mutate the same `Schedule` object the main thread mutates, and only one of the two ever runs at a time by construction (it's exactly the cooperative-coroutine guarantee)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":288,"cache_read_input_tokens":24691,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":288,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":39,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"043e06b7-5210-4cf9-a4ea-42ba9f11922a","uuid":"6a2306ed-8cd4-4a93-a77b-0432b3e11f18"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":476205,"duration_api_ms":469859,"ttft_ms":2207,"time_to_request_ms":27,"num_turns":12,"result":"Both debug and release builds compile cleanly with no errors.\n\n## Summary of the implementation\n\nThe C library implements stackful coroutines using `ucontext_t` (i.e., OS-level user-space context switching). Since Rust has no safe equivalent, and we cannot modify the `Coroutine`/`Schedule` struct definitions to embed thread/channel state, I implemented stackful coroutines using OS threads + `Mutex`/`Condvar`, with per-coroutine sync state held in a global side-table keyed by `(schedule_address, coroutine_id)`.\n\nKey points:\n\n- **`coroutine_open`** — allocates a `Schedule` with `nco=0`, `cap=DEFAULT_COROUTINE`, `running=-1`, and a vector of 16 `None` slots.\n- **`coroutine_new`** — mirrors the C allocation policy exactly: doubles capacity when full (placing the new coroutine at the old `cap`), otherwise scans starting from `(i + nco) % cap`.\n- **`coroutine_resume`** — asserts the schedule is not currently running another coroutine; for `READY`, spawns a worker thread that runs `func(schedule, data)` then cleans up the slot (`co[id] = None`, `nco--`, `running = -1`) before signalling `Finished`; for `SUSPEND`, signals the existing worker thread to resume. Either way, the main thread parks on the condvar until the worker yields or finishes.\n- **`coroutine_yield`** — sets `status = SUSPEND`, `running = -1`, signals the main thread, and parks until the next resume.\n- **`coroutine_status`** / **`coroutine_running`** — straightforward field reads, with `None` slot returning `COROUTINE_DEAD`.\n- **`coroutine_close`** — drops all per-schedule registry entries; finished workers are joined, parked workers are detached (since they cannot be unparked safely once the schedule disappears, but in normal use everything is finished by then — same as the C version which assumes all coroutines have completed before close).\n\nA small amount of `unsafe` is used for the `*mut Schedule` raw pointer that the worker thread needs (with a `Send` wrapper). This is unavoidable — the worker thread has to be able to mutate the same `Schedule` object the main thread mutates, and only one of the two ever runs at a time by construction (it's exactly the cooperative-coroutine guarantee).","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"043e06b7-5210-4cf9-a4ea-42ba9f11922a","total_cost_usd":1.1877109999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":16,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15246,"cache_read_input_tokens":208137,"output_tokens":39531,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15246},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"global.anthropic.claude-opus-4-7[1m]":{"inputTokens":16,"outputTokens":39531,"cacheReadInputTokens":208137,"cacheCreationInputTokens":15246,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.1877109999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"059675cf-32a0-422b-87ff-82f55fca43e6"}
